# WooCommerce Local Pickup Costs Extension - Cursor Rules

## Project Type
This is a WooCommerce plugin/extension for local pickup cost management.

## Core Technologies
- **WordPress**: Latest stable version
- **WooCommerce**: Latest stable version (requires WooCommerce 3.0+)
- **PHP**: 7.4+ (use modern PHP features when appropriate)
- **JavaScript**: ES6+ for any frontend enhancements
- **HTML/CSS**: Standard web technologies

## Code Quality Standards

### PHP Standards
- Follow WordPress PHP Coding Standards
- Use strict typing when possible: `declare(strict_types=1);`
- Implement proper PHPDoc blocks for all functions, classes, and methods
- Use descriptive function and variable names (camelCase for functions, snake_case for variables in WordPress context)
- Prefer dependency injection over global state
- Use early returns to reduce nesting
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- Use WordPress debug logging: `error_log()` or `WC_Logger` for debugging

### WordPress Best Practices
- Use WordPress hooks (actions and filters) instead of modifying core files
- Implement proper nonce verification for all form submissions
- Sanitize all inputs using `sanitize_text_field()`, `sanitize_email()`, etc.
- Escape all outputs using `esc_html()`, `esc_attr()`, `esc_url()`, etc.
- Use WordPress database abstraction layer (`$wpdb`) with prepared statements
- Implement proper user capability checks: `current_user_can()`
- Use `wp_enqueue_script()` and `wp_enqueue_style()` for proper asset management
- Follow WordPress naming conventions for functions and classes

### WooCommerce Best Practices
- Check for WooCommerce activation before executing WooCommerce-specific code
- Use WooCommerce's built-in functions instead of reinventing the wheel
- Leverage WooCommerce hooks (actions and filters) for extensibility
- Use WooCommerce's Settings API for plugin configuration
- Integrate settings into WooCommerce's admin interface
- Use WooCommerce session handling when needed
- Use `WC()->session->set()` and `WC()->session->get()` for session data
- Implement proper WooCommerce data stores when extending functionality
- Use WooCommerce's logging system: `wc_get_logger()->debug()`
- Support WooCommerce's translation functions: `__()`, `esc_html__()`, `sprintf()`

## Plugin Structure

### File Organization
```
woocommerce-local-pickup-costs/
├── woocommerce-local-pickup-costs.php (Main plugin file)
├── includes/
│   ├── class-wc-local-pickup-costs.php
│   ├── admin/
│   │   ├── class-admin.php
│   │   └── class-admin-settings.php
│   ├── frontend/
│   │   └── class-frontend.php
│   └── includes/
│       └── class-core-functions.php
├── assets/
│   ├── css/
│   ├── js/
│   └── images/
├── languages/
├── templates/
└── README.md
```

### Naming Conventions
- Plugin prefix: `wc_lpc` (WC Local Pickup Costs)
- Function prefix: `wc_lpc_`
- Class prefix: `WC_LPC_` or `WC_Local_Pickup_Costs_`
- Database table prefix: `wc_lpc_`
- Option prefix: `wc_lpc_`
- Transient prefix: `wc_lpc_`
- Hook prefix: `wc_lpc_`
- Use kebab-case for filenames: `class-admin-settings.php`

### Class Structure
- Use proper namespacing or class prefixes
- Document all public methods
- Use proper visibility (public, private, protected)
- Implement singleton pattern for main plugin class when appropriate
- Use dependency injection where possible

## Security Requirements

### Data Validation and Sanitization
- Always validate and sanitize user inputs
- Use WordPress sanitization functions: `sanitize_text_field()`, `sanitize_email()`, etc.
- Use `wp_unslash()` when getting data from `$_POST` or `$_GET`
- Never trust user input
- Validate data types and ranges

### Output Escaping
- Always escape output: `esc_html()`, `esc_attr()`, `esc_url()`, etc.
- Use `wp_kses()` for allowed HTML tags
- Use `esc_html_e()` for translatable strings

### Nonce Verification
- Implement nonce verification for all forms: `wp_nonce_field()`, `check_admin_referer()`
- Use nonces in AJAX requests

### Capability Checks
- Check user capabilities: `current_user_can()`, `current_user_can('manage_woocommerce')`
- Verify user is logged in when necessary: `is_user_logged_in()`

### SQL Injection Prevention
- Use `$wpdb->prepare()` for all database queries
- Never concatenate user input directly into SQL queries
- Use WordPress database abstraction layer

## WooCommerce Integration

### Shipping Method Integration
- This plugin extends WooCommerce shipping functionality
- Hook into appropriate WooCommerce shipping hooks
- Use `woocommerce_shipping_methods` filter to register custom shipping methods (if needed)
- Use `woocommerce_package_rates` filter to modify shipping rates
- Store settings using `woocommerce_shipping_settings` or custom options

### Settings Integration
- Add settings under WooCommerce > Settings > Shipping
- Use `woocommerce_shipping_instance_settings_values` filter for instance settings
- Create admin settings page if needed
- Use WooCommerce's Settings API

### Order Integration
- Store pickup cost data in order meta when appropriate
- Use `woocommerce_checkout_update_order_meta` action
- Display pickup cost information in order admin area
- Use `woocommerce_admin_order_data_after_shipping_address` to display additional data

### Cart and Checkout Integration
- Modify cart totals using appropriate WooCommerce filters
- Use `woocommerce_cart_calculate_fees` to add fees
- Validate during checkout using `woocommerce_checkout_process`
- Use `woocommerce_after_shipping_rate` to display additional information

### Translation Support
- Use WordPress translation functions: `__()`, `_e()`, `esc_html__()`, etc.
- Provide proper text domain: `'woocommerce-local-pickup-costs'`
- Use placeholder format for translatable strings with variables
- Prepare .pot file for translations

## Performance Considerations
- Use WordPress transients API for caching when appropriate
- Minimize database queries
- Use `update_option()` and `get_option()` efficiently
- Implement proper caching where needed
- Use WordPress's built-in optimization features

## Debugging and Development
- Enable WordPress debug mode for development
- Use proper logging: `error_log()` or WooCommerce logger
- Implement comprehensive error handling
- Use proper PHP error reporting levels
- Test on staging environment before production

## Version Control
- Follow semantic versioning (x.y.z)
- Keep a changelog
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Don't commit compiled files or sensitive data
- Keep .gitignore updated

## Testing
- Test all major user flows
- Test with different WooCommerce versions
- Test with various themes
- Test with other common WooCommerce extensions
- Implement unit tests where possible using PHPUnit

## Documentation
- Document all functions with proper PHPDoc
- Keep README.md updated
- Include inline comments for complex logic
- Provide user-facing documentation
- Document hook usage and customization options

## Licensing
- Follow appropriate WordPress/GPL licensing guidelines
- Include proper license headers in all files

## Common Patterns

### Plugin Header
```php
<?php
/**
 * Plugin Name: WooCommerce Local Pickup Costs
 * Plugin URI: https://example.com/woocommerce-local-pickup-costs
 * Description: Add customizable costs for local pickup shipping method.
 * Version: 1.0.0
 * Author: Your Name
 * Author URI: https://example.com
 * Text Domain: woocommerce-local-pickup-costs
 * Domain Path: /languages
 * Requires at least: 5.8
 * Tested up to: 6.4
 * Requires PHP: 7.4
 * WC requires at least: 3.0
 * WC tested up to: 8.0
 * License: GPL v2 or later
 * License URI: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-2.0.html
 */
```

### Main Plugin Class Structure
- Implement singleton pattern for main class
- Use proper activation/deactivation hooks
- Implement dependency checks (WooCommerce version)
- Register activation and deactivation hooks
- Enqueue scripts and styles on appropriate pages
- Hook into WooCommerce actions and filters

